<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════ §01 ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>The Three Cracks in Post-Training</h2>
<p>Every LLM post-training pipeline leans on three tools: Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), Reinforcement Learning (RL), and On-Policy Distillation (OPD). Each solves something the others don't — but each also breaks something. Together they leave a gap that none of the three can close alone.</p>

<p><strong>SFT</strong> trains on expert demonstrations via maximum likelihood: for each position <em>t</em>, maximize the probability of the human-written token <em>y<sub>t</sub></em> given the preceding ground-truth tokens <em>y<sub>&lt;t</sub></em>. This creates a fundamental mismatch: at training time, the model conditions on <em>human</em> tokens; at inference time, it conditions on its <em>own</em> tokens. One wrong token early in generation shifts the context into a distribution the model has never trained on, and errors compound.</p>

<p><strong>RL (GRPO)</strong> fixes the mismatch by training on the model's own rollouts. But it assigns a single scalar reward to the entire sequence — every token in a long chain-of-thought gets credit for the final answer, even the early "Let me think" tokens that had nothing to do with success or failure. When all rollouts in a batch are uniformly correct (or wrong), the advantage is zero and the gradient vanishes entirely.</p>

<p><strong>OPD (On-Policy Distillation)</strong> gives dense, per-token supervision on the student's own rollouts — fixing RL's sparse-reward problem. But it requires a separate, significantly more powerful teacher model running alongside the student during training. That doubles GPU memory. And if the teacher is too powerful, the student cannot approximate its complex distribution at all.</p>

