<section id="s-core" data-code="core">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Core Idea</div>
<h2>The Dual-Role Insight</h2>
<p>OPSD's key observation is simple: <em>evaluation is easier than generation</em>. A model given the correct answer can construct a much better reasoning trace than one generating blindly. The privileged context of knowing the answer lets the model "look back" and produce a coherent, step-by-step justification.</p>
<p>This asymmetry is the engine of OPSD. Rather than deploying a separate, larger teacher model, OPSD splits a single model into two roles using different input contexts:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Student</strong> — sees only the question <em>x</em>. Generates rollouts on-policy: <em>y ~ π<sub>θ</sub><sup>stud</sup>(·|x)</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Teacher</strong> — sees the question <em>x</em> plus the ground-truth answer <em>y*</em> (privileged information). At each position <em>t</em>, produces the distribution <em>π<sub>θ</sub><sup>teach</sup>(·|x, y*, y<sub>&lt;t</sub>)</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both roles use <em>the same parameters θ</em>. The student generates trajectories, the teacher conditions on the correct answer to provide dense per-token supervision over those exact trajectories. Then gradient descent updates θ, making both the student and teacher smarter simultaneously.</p>

