<p>The OPSD training objective is a per-token KL divergence between the student and teacher, averaged over trajectories sampled from the student itself:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">OPSD Loss (Eq. 6)</div>
  <span class="math-var">L</span><sub>OPSD</sub>(θ) = 𝔼<sub>(x,y*)~D, y~π<sub>θ</sub><sup>stud</sup>(·|x)</sub> [ <span class="math-var">Σ<sub>t=1</sub><sup>|y|</sup></span> KL( π<sub>θ</sub><sup>stud</sup>(·|x, y<sub>&lt;t</sub>) ‖ π<sub>θ</sub><sup>teach</sup>(·|x, y*, y<sub>&lt;t</sub>) ) ]
  <br><span class="math-comment">  — both roles share parameters θ; y is sampled from the student (on-policy)</span>
</div>

<p>Because both roles share θ, the model cannot "cheat" by having the teacher diverge from the student arbitrarily. It must find parameters where its blind behavior (student) closely tracks its informed behavior (teacher) — meaning its reasoning chain should look like what a version of itself that knew the answer would produce.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════ §03 ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-loop" data-code="loop">
<div class="section-no">03 — Implementation</div>
<h2>The OPSD Training Loop</h2>
<p>In practice, OPSD runs a four-step cycle per batch. The student generates rollouts; the teacher conditions on the ground-truth answer to provide per-token distributions; the KL loss flows back through shared weights. Click through the steps below.</p>

<div class="step-list" id="step-list-loop">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Sample rollout from student</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Draw a prompt-answer pair (x, y*) from dataset D. Let the student generate a rollout y by sampling autoregressively, conditioned only on x. This is the "on-policy" part — training data comes from the current model, not a static corpus.</div>
      <div class="step-code">y ~ π_θ^stud(·|x)  # on-policy rollout, no y* visible</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Teacher conditions on privileged answer</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each token position t in the rollout y, feed the teacher the same prompt x, the ground-truth answer y*, and the rollout prefix y<sub>&lt;t</sub>. The teacher produces a sharp distribution over the vocabulary — it knows where the reasoning is heading.</div>
      <div class="step-code">p_T(·|x, y*, y_&lt;t) for each t=1...|y|  # informed teacher</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Compute per-token divergence</div>
      <div class="step-desc">At each position t, measure the KL (or JSD) divergence between the student's distribution and the teacher's. High divergence at position t means: "the teacher (who knows the answer) would assign very different token probabilities here than the student does blindly."</div>
      <div class="step-code">loss_t = KL(p_S(·|x, y_&lt;t) ‖ p_T(·|x, y*, y_&lt;t))</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Update shared parameters θ</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Average the per-token losses over the rollout and over the batch. Backpropagate through the student (gradients stop at teacher outputs — teacher is treated as a "frozen" oracle for this step). Both student and teacher improve in the next iteration because θ is shared.</div>
      <div class="step-code">L = mean_t(loss_t);  θ ← θ − α ∇_θ L  # shared update</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev-loop" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span id="step-prog-loop" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 4</span>
  <button id="step-next-loop">Next →</button>
</div>

<h3>Memory advantage over OPD</h3>
<p>In standard OPD you run two separate model instances during training: one forward pass through the student to generate rollouts, then another forward pass through the (usually larger) teacher for supervision. OPSD collapses both into a single model with different prompt prefixes. The student and teacher contexts can often be batched together, and there is zero gradient storage for a separate teacher. Empirically, OPSD reduces GPU memory consumption by <strong>40–60%</strong> versus standard OPD — and in extreme large-model scenarios, OPD runs out of memory entirely while OPSD remains feasible.</p>
</section>

