<h3>Three variants worth knowing in depth</h3>

<p><strong>DASD — Direction-Adaptive Self-Distillation.</strong> Standard OPSD applies the same "pull toward teacher" supervision to every token. But some tokens are high-entropy (genuinely ambiguous — multiple reasonable next tokens exist), and others are low-entropy (the correct next step is obvious). DASD routes these differently: at high-entropy positions, it <em>pushes the student away</em> from the teacher to preserve exploratory diversity; at low-entropy positions, it <em>pulls the student toward</em> the teacher for accuracy. On six math reasoning benchmarks, DASD achieves the best macro Avg@16 score among RLVR and self-distillation baselines.</p>

<p><strong>ROSD — Reflective On-Policy Self-Distillation.</strong> Instead of conditioning the teacher on the ground-truth solution (which encourages copying), ROSD uses a self-reflector that first identifies where in the student's rollout reasoning first breaks down, then conditions the teacher on a corrective idea targeting that specific span. Distillation is then restricted to just the erroneous span — preserving valid reasoning prefixes and preventing the teacher from overwriting correct steps. Result: stronger in-domain reasoning and substantially better out-of-domain generalization than standard OPSD.</p>

<p><strong>OPSA — Safety Alignment without Penalizing Reasoning.</strong> Safety alignment traditionally hurts reasoning capability — the "safety tax." The problem is off-policy training mismatch: safety demonstrations come from humans or external models, not the model's own distribution. OPSA applies OPSD specifically for safety: the teacher is given a "privileged safety context" that activates the model's latent safety reasoning, while the student sees only the harmful query. The "teacher flip rate" metric identifies which safety contexts are genuinely activating latent safety rather than surface-level refusals. Gains: +8.85 points on R1-Distill-1.5B, +5.49 points on Qwen3-0.6B, with reasoning capability preserved.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════ §05 ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-signal" data-code="signal">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Key Variable</div>
<h2>Tuning the Divergence: β and the JSD Dial</h2>
<p>OPSD measures the gap between the student and teacher using a divergence metric. The choice of metric is a key design variable, controlled by a parameter <strong>β ∈ [0, 1]</strong> in the generalized Jensen-Shannon divergence (JSD):</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Generalized JSD (Eq. 7–8)</div>
  JSD<sub>β</sub>(p<sub>T</sub> ‖ p<sub>S</sub>) = β · D<sub>KL</sub>(p<sub>T</sub> ‖ m) + (1−β) · D<sub>KL</sub>(p<sub>S</sub> ‖ m)<br>
  <span class="math-comment">  where  m = β·p<sub>T</sub> + (1−β)·p<sub>S</sub>  (mixture distribution)</span><br><br>
  <span class="math-comment">  β→0 : reverse KL (mode-seeking: student commits to one high-quality mode)</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">  β=0.5 : symmetric JSD (balanced; bounded in [0, log 2], stable gradients)</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">  β→1 : forward KL (mode-covering: student must match all teacher modes)</span>
</div>

<p>The β knob interpolates between two extremes. <strong>Reverse KL (β→0)</strong> is mode-seeking: the student avoids assigning probability to tokens the teacher considers unlikely, which produces sharp, decisive answers — but may miss alternative correct reasoning paths. <strong>Forward KL (β→1)</strong> is mode-covering: the student must match every mode of the teacher's distribution, including low-probability ones, encouraging diversity at the cost of precision. The <strong>symmetric JSD (β=0.5)</strong> is the commonly used default: it is bounded between 0 and log 2, preventing gradient explosions, and the mixture distribution acts as a smoothing bridge between the two extremes.</p>

